### How does ingestion work in a fraud platform?

Ingestion starts with Kafka as a durable, partitioned log, partitioned by account or card so one entity's events stay ordered and consumers scale out. The semantics choice matters: at-least-once is simpler but can reprocess an event, while exactly-once guarantees each event is scored once.

```java
package com.bank.fraud;

import java.util.Properties;

public class FraudIngestion {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "kafka:9092");
        props.put("acks", "all");           // durability
        props.put("enable.idempotence", "true"); // exactly-once
        props.put("transactional.id", "fraud-producer-1");

        System.out.println("Kafka producer config:");
        System.out.println("  acks=all: wait for all replicas");
        System.out.println("  idempotent: no duplicates");
        System.out.println("  transactional: exactly-once");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Partitioned by card_id:");
        System.out.println("  All swipes for one card in order.");
    }
}
```

```text
Kafka producer config:
  acks=all: wait for all replicas
  idempotent: no duplicates
  transactional: exactly-once

Partitioned by card_id:
  All swipes for one card in order.
```

### How does streaming fraud detection work?

The streaming detection engine is a stateful Flink or Spark job. For each event it performs feature lookups: recent spending velocity, typical locations, average amount, and device history, from keyed state and a fast feature store like Redis. It then applies rules and a model to produce a score and decision.

```java
package com.bank.fraud;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class FraudScorer {
    record Swipe(String cardId, BigDecimal amount,
                 String city, long timestamp) {}
    record Features(BigDecimal avgAmount, String usualCity,
                    int txnsLastMinute) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Swipe swipe = new Swipe("CARD-2001",
            new BigDecimal("95000"), "Mumbai", System.currentTimeMillis());
        Features features = new Features(
            new BigDecimal("15000"), "Delhi", 2);

        // Rule 1: amount >> average
        boolean highAmount = swipe.amount().compareTo(
            features.avgAmount().multiply(new BigDecimal("5"))) > 0;
        // Rule 2: unusual city
        boolean unusualCity = !swipe.city().equals(
            features.usualCity());
        // Rule 3: velocity spike
        boolean velocitySpike = features.txnsLastMinute() > 3;

        String decision;
        if (highAmount && unusualCity) {
            decision = "BLOCK";
        } else if (highAmount || velocitySpike) {
            decision = "CHALLENGE";
        } else {
            decision = "ALLOW";
        }
        System.out.println("Card: " + swipe.cardId());
        System.out.println("Decision: " + decision);
    }
}
```

```text
Card: CARD-2001
Decision: BLOCK
```

### How does the storage split work?

An OLTP store serves the live decision path and current balances with low latency. An OLAP store or data lake receives a copy of all events and decisions for analytics, investigation, and model training, isolated so heavy workloads never slow the real-time path.

```java
package com.bank.fraud;

public class StorageSplit {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("OLTP (PostgreSQL):");
        System.out.println("  Live card balances");
        System.out.println("  Fraud decisions (allow/block)");
        System.out.println("  Latency: < 10 ms");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("OLAP (Redshift / Lake):");
        System.out.println("  All events + decisions (copy)");
        System.out.println("  Model training data");
        System.out.println("  Investigation queries");
        System.out.println("  Isolated from real-time path");
    }
}
```

```text
OLTP (PostgreSQL):
  Live card balances
  Fraud decisions (allow/block)
  Latency: < 10 ms

OLAP (Redshift / Lake):
  All events + decisions (copy)
  Model training data
  Investigation queries
  Isolated from real-time path
```

### How do you ensure reliability?

Schema validation via a registry stops malformed producers. Per-event data validation checks nulls, ranges, and invariants. Poison records go to a dead-letter queue so one bad message never blocks a partition. Monitoring tracks lag, latency, decision rates, and drift.

```java
package com.bank.fraud;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ReliabilityControls {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Schema validation
        System.out.println("Schema registry: reject incompatible changes");

        // Data validation
        BigDecimal amount = null;
        if (amount == null || amount.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) <= 0) {
            System.out.println("Validation: null amount -> DLQ");
        }

        // Monitoring
        System.out.println("Monitor:");
        System.out.println("  Consumer lag: < 1000 events");
        System.out.println("  Scoring latency: p99 < 50 ms");
        System.out.println("  Block rate: alert if > 5%");
        System.out.println("  DLQ rate: alert if > 0.1%");
    }
}
```

```text
Schema registry: reject incompatible changes
Validation: null amount -> DLQ
Monitor:
  Consumer lag: < 1000 events
  Scoring latency: p99 < 50 ms
  Block rate: alert if > 5%
  DLQ rate: alert if > 0.1%
```

### What is the safer-but-slower trade-off?

A bank deliberately prefers safer-but-slightly-slower choices: exactly-once over at-least-once, block or challenge on uncertainty rather than approve, quarantine questionable records rather than process blindly, and spend extra milliseconds on feature lookups for a better decision.

```java
package com.bank.fraud;

public class SaferButSlower {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Bank trade-offs (safer-but-slower):");
        System.out.println("  1. Exactly-once > at-least-once");
        System.out.println("     (more overhead, no duplicates)");
        System.out.println("  2. Block on uncertainty > approve");
        System.out.println("     (false positive < false negative)");
        System.out.println("  3. Quarantine > process blindly");
        System.out.println("     (DLQ over silent corruption)");
        System.out.println("  4. Feature lookup > skip for speed");
        System.out.println("     (better decision > faster decision)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Constraint: scoring time budget.");
        System.out.println("  If budget exceeded, safe default (CHALLENGE).");
    }
}
```

```text
Bank trade-offs (safer-but-slower):
  1. Exactly-once > at-least-once
     (more overhead, no duplicates)
  2. Block on uncertainty > approve
     (false positive < false negative)
  3. Quarantine > process blindly
     (DLQ over silent corruption)
  4. Feature lookup > skip for speed
     (better decision > faster decision)

Constraint: scoring time budget.
  If budget exceeded, safe default (CHALLENGE).
```

### How does the scoring time budget work?

Fraud systems must bound their own latency so a legitimate payment is not delayed too long. You set a strict scoring time budget with a safe default decision if the budget is exceeded, balancing safety with customer experience.

```java
package com.bank.fraud;

public class ScoringTimeBudget {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long budgetMs = 100;  // 100 ms max
        long start = System.currentTimeMillis();

        // Simulate scoring work
        String decision = "ALLOW"; // default
        long elapsed = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;

        if (elapsed > budgetMs) {
            decision = "CHALLENGE"; // safe default
            System.out.println("Budget exceeded (" + elapsed + " ms)."
                + " Defaulting to CHALLENGE.");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Scored in " + elapsed + " ms."
                + " Decision: " + decision);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Scored in 0 ms. Decision: ALLOW
```

### What are the end-to-end platform components?

The full platform has five layers: ingestion (Kafka), scoring (Flink), serving (OLTP), analytics (OLAP/lake), and reliability (validation, DLQ, monitoring). Every decision is idempotent so a replay never double-applies a block.

```java
package com.bank.fraud;

public class PlatformComponents {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Fraud Platform Layers:");
        System.out.println("  1. Ingestion: Kafka (exactly-once)");
        System.out.println("  2. Scoring: Flink (stateful, features)");
        System.out.println("  3. Serving: PostgreSQL (live decisions)");
        System.out.println("  4. Analytics: Redshift (investigation)");
        System.out.println("  5. Reliability: registry, DLQ, monitoring");
    }
}
```

```text
Fraud Platform Layers:
  1. Ingestion: Kafka (exactly-once)
  2. Scoring: Flink (stateful, features)
  3. Serving: PostgreSQL (live decisions)
  4. Analytics: Redshift (investigation)
  5. Reliability: registry, DLQ, monitoring
```

